-## Subtopics
-
-### MLP-based MS-NeRF
-Applies the multi-space module to MLP-based backbones like Mip-NeRF 360. It improves PSNR by 4.15 dB on reflective regions [S1]. [See MS-NeRF](12996-ms-nerf.md)
-
-### Grid-based MS-NeRF
-Applies the multi-space module to grid-based backbones like TensoRF. It improves PSNR by 2.71 dB on reflective regions [S1]. [See MS-NeRF](12996-ms-nerf.md)
+*   The method requires no additional loss terms compared to vanilla NeRF, relying on standard photometric loss [S1].
+*   Computational overhead is small for training and inference [S1].
+*   The composition weights of sub-spaces change dynamically according to spatial location and view direction, allowing the model to handle complex light paths [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** The method introduces small computational overheads for training and inference. The exact complexity increase is not quantified beyond the parameter count [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   On Mip-NeRF 360: +4.15 dB PSNR on reflective regions with 0.5% extra parameters [S1].
-    *   On TensoRF: +2.71 dB PSNR on reflective regions with 0.046% extra parameters [S1].
-    *   No degradation in rendering quality on non-reflective regions [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Automatically handles mirror-like objects in 360-degree rendering without manual labeling; overcomes multi-view consistency violations by placing virtual images in view-dependent sub-spaces [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires the construction of a novel dataset for complex reflection/refraction evaluation (33 synthetic, 7 real scenes) as existing datasets were insufficient [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering scenes with complex reflections and refractions where multi-view consistency is violated in Euclidean space [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** For scenes without reflective/refractive objects, the added complexity may be unnecessary given the marginal parameter cost [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On the Mip-NeRF 360 dataset, MS-NeRF improves PSNR by 4.15 dB on reflective regions with only 0.5% extra parameters, without degrading rendering quality on other regions [S1]. On TensoRF, it improves PSNR by 2.71 dB on reflective regions with 0.046% extra parameters [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The method introduces small computational overheads for training and inferring the extra-space outputs [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Automatically handles mirror-like objects in 360-degree rendering without manual labeling; overcomes multi-view consistency violations by placing virtual images in specific sub-spaces; works as a general enhancement for both MLP-based (NeRF, Mip-NeRF, Mip-NeRF 360) and grid-based (TensoRF, iNGP) methods [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance on non-reflective regions is not degraded but not improved beyond the backbone; requires careful tuning of $K$ and $d$ [S1].
 
 ## Background
-MS-NeRF was proposed by Z.X. Yin, P.Y. Jiao, M.M. Cheng, B. Ren, and J. Qiu [S1]. A preliminary version appeared at CVPR [S1]. The full version was published in IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence in 2025 [S1]. The authors constructed a novel dataset of 33 synthetic and 7 real captured scenes with complex camera paths to benchmark robustness, as existing datasets like RFFR and Shiny lacked sufficient viewpoint changes or 360-degree coverage for mirror-like objects [S1].
+MS-NeRF was proposed to address the limitations of existing NeRF methods in handling reflective and refractive objects, which often result in blurry or distorted rendering due to violated multi-view consistency in Euclidean space [S1]. Existing datasets like RFFR and Shiny lacked sufficient coverage of 360-degree rendering with mirror-like objects, prompting the construction of a novel dataset with 33 synthetic and 7 real captured scenes [S1]. The method was published in IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence in 2025, with a preliminary version appearing at CVPR [S1].
 
